\end{table}

Fine-tuned \cls{} achieves a mean accuracy of 88.43\% ($\pm$0.78 across seeds 87.83, 89.52, 87.88, 89.00, 87.94), compared to 87.19\% ($\pm$0.36; seeds: 87.51, 87.20, 86.98, 86.67, 87.57) for \cwm{} and 86.30\% ($\pm$0.97; seeds: 85.66, 85.47, 87.40, 87.31, 85.66) for TRAJ. The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap is 1.24\pp{}, which is modest at this complexity level---both formulations operate on short, single-function code with simple return values, where generation is not yet bottlenecked by token budget or string complexity.

Zero-shot baselines lag by a wide margin: Qwen3-8B achieves 64.40\% and Qwen3-32B (NF4) achieves 71.69\%, both far below fine-tuned 4B models. The 8B zero-shot model exhibits a strong FAIL bias (fail recall 95.8\%, pass recall 35.1\%), indicating that zero-shot prompting cannot achieve the balanced precision/recall that fine-tuning produces. Even with 8$\times$ more parameters, the 32B zero-shot model trails the fine-tuned 4B \cls{} by 16.74\pp{}, confirming that task-specific fine-tuning, rather than model scale alone, is necessary for reliable per-test verification.


\subsection{Scaling Behavior}
\label{sec:scaling}

Table~\ref{tab:scaling} compares \cls{} and \cwm{} at two model sizes using three matched seeds (42, 123, 456), all trained in full bfloat16 precision to ensure a controlled comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Model-size scaling: \cls{} vs.\ \cwm{} accuracy (\%) at 4B and 8B. Three matched seeds (42, 123, 456) in full precision. The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap widens from 1.18\pp{} to 2.43\pp{}.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Size} & \textbf{Seed 42} & \textbf{Seed 123} & \textbf{Seed 456} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Gap} \\
\midrule
\cls{} & 4B & 87.83 & 89.52 & 87.88 & 88.41 & \multirow{2}{*}{1.18} \\
\cwm{} & 4B & 87.51 & 87.20 & 86.98 & 87.23 & \\
\midrule
\cls{} & 8B & \textbf{90.48} & 89.40 & 88.31 & \textbf{89.40} & \multirow{2}{*}{\textbf{2.43}} \\
\cwm{} & 8B & 87.67 & 86.98 & 86.24 & 86.96 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At 4B, \cls{} and \cwm{} are separated by 1.18\pp{} on the matched three-seed subset. At 8B, the gap more than doubles to 2.43\pp{}. Clustered bootstrap analysis yields a mean gap of 2.26\pp{} ($p = 0.0066$, 95\% CI $[1.80, 2.71]$), confirming the widening is not a sampling artifact.

The asymmetry in scaling efficiency is striking: from 4B to 8B, \cls{} gains +0.99\pp{} (88.41 $\to$ 89.40) while \cwm{} loses $-$0.27\pp{} (87.23 $\to$ 86.96). The additional model capacity benefits classification---where the model needs only to discriminate pass from fail---but provides no measurable improvement for generation, where the model must additionally produce exact output strings. This pattern is consistent with the hypothesis that the classification advantage is complexity-dependent: generation encounters diminishing returns earlier because the string-prediction bottleneck, not model capacity, bounds its accuracy. Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap} visualizes the diverging trajectories.


\subsection{Per-Test vs.\ Trajectory-Level Equivalence}
\label{sec:equivalence}

A natural concern is whether per-test predictions, when aggregated to the variant level, match the accuracy of a model trained directly at that granularity. We address this by comparing \cls{} (aggregated via all-pass rule) against TRAJ on 523 variant-level instances across five seeds.

The pooled gap is $-$0.45\pp{} (95\% CI [$-$1.71, +0.84]), with per-seed gaps ranging from $-$1.84 to +1.45\pp{} (seed 42: $-$0.97; seed 123: +1.45; seed 456: $-$1.84; seed 789: $-$0.78; seed 0: $-$0.29). The TOST equivalence test rejects the null hypothesis of non-equivalence at $p = 0.007$ with a $\pm$2\pp{} margin, confirming that per-test classification and trajectory-level scoring achieve statistically equivalent variant-level accuracy.

Critically, however, predictions within the same problem are not independent. The intraclass correlation coefficient is ICC $= 0.191$ ($p < 0.0001$), yielding a design effect DEFF $= 3.97$. This means that naive i.i.d.\ confidence intervals would be approximately $2\times$ too narrow. All statistical tests in this paper use clustered bootstrap to account for this within-problem correlation.

The practical implication is clear: per-test classification provides per-test diagnostic information---which specific tests fail and why---at no cost to variant-level accuracy. A trajectory-level model gives only a binary patch verdict, discarding the per-test signal that downstream repair systems could exploit for targeted re-repair.


\subsection{Accuracy-Utility Frontier}
\label{sec:oracle}

Where does per-test granularity actually help? We formalize this via an oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified, stratifying instances by the number of F2P tests $T$ and comparing per-test vs.\ trajectory-level oracle resolve rates under both i.i.d.\ and block-correlated error models (using the empirical ICC $= 0.191$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Oracle accuracy-utility frontier: per-test advantage stratified by F2P test count ($T$) on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances). Block-correlated model uses empirical ICC $= 0.191$. P2P regression rate: 1.04\% mean, 0\% median.}
\label{tab:oracle}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{$T$ Bin} & \textbf{Inst.\%} & \textbf{i.i.d.\ Adv.\!(\pp{})} & \textbf{Block Adv.\!(\pp{})} & \textbf{Contrib.\%} \\
\midrule
$T = 1$ & 68.7 & $-$0.04 & $-$0.09 & $\approx$0 \\
$T = 2$ & 19.1 & 18.49 & 31.69 & 55.3 \\
$T = 3$--$5$ & 7.1 & 25.00 & 40.74 & $\approx$26 \\
$T \geq 6$ & 5.1 & 30.00 & 40.06 & $\approx$19 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Overall} & 100.0 & --- & \textbf{10.93} & 100.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:oracle} reveals a sharp concentration of per-test advantage. When $T = 1$---the dominant case at 68.7\% of SWE-bench instances---per-test and trajectory-level are informationally equivalent (block advantage $-$0.09\pp{}), because a single F2P test provides no more signal than a binary patch verdict. The advantage materializes at $T = 2$ (19.1\% of instances), where block-correlated advantage reaches 31.69\pp{}, contributing 55.3\% of the total advantage. Instances with $T \geq 3$ exhibit block advantages exceeding 40\pp{} but constitute only 12.2\% of instances. The overall block-correlated advantage is 10.93\pp{}, driven almost entirely by the $T \geq 2$ minority. Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_stratified} visualizes this distribution.

Block correlation amplifies the advantage relative to the i.i.d.\ model: at $T = 2$, the block advantage (31.69\pp{}) is 1.7$\times$ the i.i.d.\ advantage (18.49\pp{}). This occurs because correlated errors make the per-test classifier more likely to get all tests correct or all wrong for a given problem, increasing the information value of each additional test.

\paragraph{P2P regression signal.}
The P2P regression rate in SWE-bench Verified is low: 1.04\% mean and 0\% median---77\% of incorrect patches break zero P2P tests. This means per-test granularity on the P2P dimension currently provides minimal signal. The practical value of per-test verification concentrates in F2P discrimination, particularly for instances with multiple F2P tests.

\subsection{Repo-Level Results}
\label{sec:repo}

Repo-level experiments on SWE-bench Verified are in progress. We are training \cls{} and \cwm{} models (Qwen3-4B, LoRA, seed 42) on repo-level per-test data with max sequence length 4,096 and class weighting to handle the 85:15 pass:fail imbalance. We hypothesize that \cwm{} failure modes---token-budget exhaustion on long execution traces and string-value prediction errors on complex output types---will emerge more acutely at repo-level complexity, widening the \cls{} advantage beyond the function-level gap. Results will be reported upon completion.

% \begin{table}[t]
% \centering
% \small
% \caption{[PLACEHOLDER] Repo-level results: \cls{} vs.\ \cwm{} on SWE-bench Verified (Qwen3-4B, seed=42).}
% \label{tab:repo_level}
% \begin{tabular}{lccc}
% \toprule
% \textbf{Method} & \textbf{Resolve Rate} & \textbf{Per-Test F1} & \textbf{Token Exhaust.\ Rate} \\
% \midrule
% \cls{} & --- & --- & --- \\
% \cwm{} & --- & --- & --- \\
% \bottomrule
% \end{tabular}
% \end{table}


\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{(a) Error complementarity.}
\cls{} and \cwm{} make errors in opposite directions. Among \cls{} errors (seed 42), 75.5\% are false negatives (FAIL $\to$ PASS), meaning the classifier tends to over-predict passing. Among \cwm{} errors, 83.1\% are false positives (PASS $\to$ FAIL), reflecting string-matching failures that incorrectly reject correct outputs. An oracle combination that takes the correct answer whenever either model is right achieves 90.63\% accuracy, a 3.12\pp{} improvement over \cls{} alone (87.51\%). This complementarity suggests that ensemble approaches combining classification and generation signals could yield further gains, though we leave this to future work.

\paragraph{(b) Per-test precision and recall.}
At the per-test level (seed 42), \cls{} achieves accuracy 87.51\%, fail \fone{} 87.86\% (precision 82.75\%, recall 93.64\%), and pass \fone{} 87.15\% (precision 93.24\%, recall 81.80\%). Of 114 problems in the test set, 48 (42.1\%) are perfectly predicted across all their tests. Performance varies by mutation type: \texttt{return\_value} is easiest (97.3\% accuracy) while \texttt{off\_by\_one} is hardest (82.6\%). Multi-F2P instances with 6--10 tests reach 96.3\% per-test accuracy, corroborating the oracle analysis finding that per-test granularity is most valuable when multiple F2P tests are available.

\paragraph{(c) Majority voting.}
Majority voting ($K = 3$, temperature 0.7) decreases accuracy from 87.51\% to 86.83\% ($-$0.68\pp{}). Of 127 split-vote samples, 28 flip from correct to incorrect while only 15 flip in the favorable direction (net loss: 13 samples). The model's errors are systematic rather than stochastic, so sampling-based ensembles do not help; single-pass greedy decoding remains optimal.

\paragraph{(d) Block correlation.}
Empirical analysis confirms that prediction errors within the same problem are correlated: ICC $= 0.191$ ($p < 0.0001$), DEFF $= 3.97$. The number of problems with all tests correctly predicted is 2.81$\times$ what would be expected under i.i.d.\ errors (46 observed vs.\ 16.35 expected). This non-independence has two implications: (1) statistical tests must account for clustering, as we do throughout; (2) the block-correlated oracle model (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) better reflects real deployment than the i.i.d.\ model, making its higher advantage estimates the more relevant ones.

