\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We formalize execution-free verification as a prediction problem over test outcomes and compare three formulations that differ in output granularity and generation complexity. We then develop an oracle framework that characterizes when per-test granularity provides value over trajectory-level scoring.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem_setup}

Consider a code repository with a candidate patch $p$ and a test suite $T = \{t_1, \ldots, t_n\}$. Tests are partitioned into two sets: $T_{\text{f2p}} \subseteq T$ (FAIL\_TO\_PASS), which fail before the patch and should pass after a correct fix, and $T_{\text{p2p}} \subseteq T$ (PASS\_TO\_PASS), which pass before the patch and should remain passing. For each test $t_i$, the goal is to predict a verdict $v_i \in \{\textsc{pass}, \textsc{fail}\}$ without executing the code. A patch is correct if and only if all F2P tests pass and all P2P tests pass:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:patch_correct}
\text{correct}(p) \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \bigwedge_{t_i \in T_{\text{f2p}}} (v_i = \textsc{pass}) \;\wedge\; \bigwedge_{t_j \in T_{\text{p2p}}} (v_j = \textsc{pass}).
\end{equation}

The central question is \emph{how} an LLM should predict $v_i$. We compare three formulations that span the design space of output granularity (per-test vs.\ per-patch) and output complexity (classification vs.\ generation).

\subsection{Per-Test Binary Classification (\cls{})}
\label{sec:cls}

The classification formulation maps a code context $c$ and a single test $t_i$ to a binary verdict:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cls}
f(c, t_i) \;\to\; \{\textsc{pass}, \textsc{fail}\}.
\end{equation}
The model produces a single token per test, trained with standard cross-entropy loss on binary labels derived from execution. At inference, each test requires one forward pass; the patch-level verdict follows from Equation~\ref{eq:patch_correct}.

The output space is fixed and minimal by construction. There is no variable-length decoding, no dependence on output string complexity, and no risk of truncation. These properties eliminate two failure modes that affect generation-based alternatives (\S\ref{sec:cwm_method}).

\subsection{Execution Output Generation (\cwm{})}
\label{sec:cwm_method}

The generation formulation maps a code context and test to the execution output string:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cwm}
g(c, t_i) \;\to\; o_i, \quad \text{where } o_i \text{ is the predicted output string.}
\end{equation}
The verdict is derived by exact match against the expected output $o_i^*$: $v_i = \textsc{pass}$ if $o_i = o_i^*$, and $\textsc{fail}$ otherwise. Unlike \cls{}, this requires generating a variable-length sequence---the output may range from a single boolean to thousands of characters for complex data structures.

This formulation inherits two failure modes from open-ended generation:

\paragraph{(a) Token-budget exhaustion.}
When the true output length $|o_i^*|$ exceeds the generation budget $B$, the predicted output is truncated: $|o_i| = B < |o_i^*|$. Even if the model correctly predicts the output prefix, exact match fails, producing a spurious $\textsc{fail}$ verdict. This failure mode is systematic: it affects all tests whose expected outputs exceed the budget, regardless of model capability.

\paragraph{(b) String-value mismatch.}
Complex return types---floating-point numbers, nested dictionaries, sets with arbitrary ordering, long formatted strings---require the model to predict exact string representations. Minor formatting differences (e.g., trailing whitespace, key ordering in dicts, floating-point precision) cause exact-match failure even when the model has correctly inferred the semantic output. This mode scales with output complexity: function-level code with simple returns is largely unaffected, while repository-level code with rich output types triggers frequent mismatches.

Both failure modes are absent in \cls{} by construction: the output is always a single token from a fixed vocabulary, independent of the complexity of the underlying execution output.

\subsection{Trajectory-Level Scoring (TRAJ)}
\label{sec:traj}

The trajectory-level formulation takes the full code context and entire test suite as input and produces a single patch-level verdict:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:traj}
h(c, T) \;\to\; \{\textsc{resolved}, \textsc{unresolved}\}.
\end{equation}
This formulation operates at the coarsest granularity: one binary prediction per patch, with no decomposition into per-test signals. It serves as a control for the granularity dimension---comparing TRAJ against \cls{} isolates whether per-test decomposition helps or hurts when the same underlying information (code context and tests) is available.

The key limitation is the absence of diagnostic signal. A TRAJ verdict of $\textsc{unresolved}$ provides no information about \emph{which} tests fail, precluding targeted re-repair strategies that could exploit per-test failure localization.

\subsection{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Framework}
\label{sec:oracle_framework}

The three formulations differ not only in accuracy but in the \emph{granularity} of their output. To formalize when per-test granularity provides value over trajectory-level scoring, we develop an oracle analysis framework parameterized by four quantities: per-test accuracy $a$, the number of F2P tests $|T_{\text{f2p}}|$, P2P regression rate $r$, and intra-problem error correlation $\rho$ (intraclass correlation coefficient, ICC).

\paragraph{The $T = 1$ equivalence.}
When a problem has a single F2P test ($|T_{\text{f2p}}| = 1$), per-test and trajectory-level are informationally equivalent---both reduce to a single binary prediction about the same test. Any advantage of per-test granularity requires $|T_{\text{f2p}}| \geq 2$.

\paragraph{Per-test advantage for $T \geq 2$.}
With multiple F2P tests, a per-test verifier can correctly identify a subset of failing tests even when some predictions are wrong. A trajectory-level verifier, by contrast, collapses all per-test information into a single bit, losing the ability to distinguish partially correct patches. The per-test advantage grows with $|T_{\text{f2p}}|$: more tests provide more opportunities for the per-test signal to be informative despite individual prediction errors.

\paragraph{Block-correlation model.}
A critical complication is that prediction errors within a problem are not independent. Tests from the same problem share code context, and model errors on one test are predictive of errors on other tests from the same problem. We model this via a block-correlation structure with ICC $= \rho$, yielding a design effect:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:deff}
\text{DEFF} = 1 + (\bar{n} - 1)\rho,
\end{equation}
where $\bar{n}$ is the mean number of tests per problem. Higher $\rho$ produces more extreme outcomes: the model tends to get all tests correct or all tests wrong for a given problem, rather than distributing errors uniformly. This amplifies the per-test advantage because block-correlated successes (all tests correct) contribute directly to resolve rate, while block-correlated failures (all tests wrong) are no worse than a trajectory-level miss.

\paragraph{Decision criterion.}
Per-test granularity provides value when: (i) $|T_{\text{f2p}}| \geq 2$, so the per-test decomposition is non-trivial; (ii) the per-test block advantage exceeds the noise floor imposed by the error rate; and (iii) P2P regression rate $r$ is high enough that per-test P2P monitoring adds signal. We evaluate these conditions empirically in \S\ref{sec:oracle} using the SWE-bench Verified test distribution and the block-correlation parameters estimated from our function-level experiments (ICC $= 0.191$, DEFF $= 3.97$).

